From: WallView
Subject: [PATCH] EventDispatcher: scroll touch drags on the scrolling thread in
 synchronous wheel event regions

WPE turns a touch drag into wheel events with gesture phases (Began,
Changed, Ended). Where the page has a non-passive wheel listener the
scrolling tree asks for synchronous processing, so such an event went to
the main thread, which scrolled and answered the UI process
(DidReceiveEventIPC). The UI process sends the next wheel event only
after that answer and pushes one more for every ten it has queued.

The existing rule that keeps a continuing gesture on the scrolling
thread only applies when the gesture started there. A drag that started
on such an element therefore depended on the main thread for every scroll
position: with a Home Assistant view that contains a chart (zrender
registers a non-passive wheel listener, and its touchmove handler takes
about 100 ms per event on the NSPanel) the main thread answered 0-6 of
the 33 touch events of a swipe while the finger was down, the UI process
sent 3-4 wheel events per swipe instead of about 30, their answers came
up to 2 s later, and about 40 % of the vblanks of a drag had no new frame
whatever the compositor did.

A wheel event with the Began phase only comes from a touch drag on WPE.
Handle the events of such a gesture, from Began to Ended, on the
scrolling thread like the ones outside a synchronous region. The wheel
event is still dispatched to the page afterwards; it can no longer block
or cancel the scroll, which matches other engines, where a touch scroll
is governed by touch events and touch-action and sends no wheel events.

Measured on the panel with a copy of the library in which the same
decision is forced (missed vblanks in the drag window, two interleaved
rounds of 8 swipes): settings 49.7 / 39.6 % -> 5.8 / 8.3 %, climate
8.3 / 14.3 -> 9.1 / 8.3, lights 3.3 / 2.0 -> 0.6 / 1.3.

WALLVIEW_WEBKIT_SYNC_TOUCH_SCROLL (any value) restores the old behaviour
for A/B measurements.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebPage/EventDispatcher.h
+++ b/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebPage/EventDispatcher.h
@@ -176,6 +176,9 @@
     HashMap<WebCore::PageIdentifier, RefPtr<WebCore::ThreadedScrollingTree>> m_scrollingTrees WTF_GUARDED_BY_LOCK(m_scrollingTreesLock);
 #endif
     std::unique_ptr<WebCore::WheelEventDeltaFilter> m_recentWheelEventDeltaFilter;
+#if PLATFORM(WPE)
+    bool m_isHandlingTouchScrollGesture { false };
+#endif
 #if ENABLE(IOS_TOUCH_EVENTS)
     Lock m_touchEventsLock;
     HashMap<WebCore::PageIdentifier, UniqueRef<TouchEventQueue>> m_touchEvents WTF_GUARDED_BY_LOCK(m_touchEventsLock);
--- a/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebPage/EventDispatcher.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebPage/EventDispatcher.cpp
@@ -168,6 +168,16 @@
             useMainThreadForScrolling = false;
 #endif
 
+#if PLATFORM(WPE)
+        static bool waitsForMainThread = !!getenv("WALLVIEW_WEBKIT_SYNC_TOUCH_SCROLL");
+        if (platformWheelEvent.phase() == PlatformWheelEventPhase::Began)
+            m_isHandlingTouchScrollGesture = true;
+        if (m_isHandlingTouchScrollGesture && !waitsForMainThread)
+            useMainThreadForScrolling = false;
+        if (platformWheelEvent.phase() == PlatformWheelEventPhase::Ended || platformWheelEvent.phase() == PlatformWheelEventPhase::Cancelled)
+            m_isHandlingTouchScrollGesture = false;
+#endif
+
         scrollingTree->willProcessWheelEvent();
 
         ScrollingThread::dispatch([scrollingTree, wheelEvent, platformWheelEvent, processingSteps, useMainThreadForScrolling, pageID, wheelEventOrigin, this, protectedThis = Ref { *this }] {
